#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "699a2e70-8eaa-5123-b840-75433e713f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269632} {:answer/id #uuid "c90f12c5-08c6-5467-84c7-cba2de905019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269633} {:answer/id #uuid "80e49bc2-ebd8-592b-8f80-190873bae453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269634} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1aef5f-d9b3-5cd5-b54a-9fa7143e59c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269635} {:answer/id #uuid "6474dc8b-100b-584c-8143-5ad2a59f18ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6474dc8b-100b-584c-8143-5ad2a59f18ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} :answer-field/id #uuid "060b8a99-9954-5d74-b747-2b4de118f6c0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c205fa0-d12d-510f-bf38-5376ffd48764" :question/math-academy-id "q-107499" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d7e182-b298-5f94-a314-a10b321bf8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269810} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f44493-de44-5faa-88f8-7da0deb55732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-2,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "0e152e1c-49fa-5f3f-8b76-4d8b625cbcd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} {:answer/id #uuid "117f14a6-a963-5d5c-a450-a45c7d8fe2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+3,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d7a8c7-a5d5-5b11-a77e-2f92af341958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3de9cf-84d9-5ffb-a26d-3666f3702b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-110799" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06553106-a12c-55fb-812c-e0b0b315f7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(511)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270147} {:answer/id #uuid "86a1de37-572c-5e18-a0da-fb5327fd0535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(529)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270148} {:answer/id #uuid "036dfa90-10a1-5de8-beab-810972593003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(511)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270149} {:answer/id #uuid "c82bb0ca-db63-58f5-a235-e41d3e9c705d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(255)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270150} {:answer/id #uuid "edc8c44c-9813-5bb4-97ea-a07db1c2b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc8c44c-9813-5bb4-97ea-a07db1c2b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a45c653-67a6-5187-b484-12b0dbb9a2f4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e33d82b-67b7-5f1d-84c4-6afcc46ec7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114209" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}1-\\operatorname{arctan}0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed1761-ceeb-5033-afa2-cb735e6bb96c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b85f6829-84a8-52bd-a851-877b050f4826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270172} {:answer/id #uuid "86aca8a2-f7fe-513f-947c-c40ad74f365e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270173} {:answer/id #uuid "c8999e92-bba2-5179-9360-ba488b206d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270174} {:answer/id #uuid "4a151041-d89e-5dbe-bf68-8e8b24859960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270175} {:answer/id #uuid "17825f5f-edbb-5a96-8348-df95f5d17098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86aca8a2-f7fe-513f-947c-c40ad74f365e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270173} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc9a344-346e-560d-9fc5-2c6bf704bb5b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" :question/math-academy-id "q-114961" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0fce35b-dea5-5fa0-915b-14d76c22527c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270192} {:answer/id #uuid "8709881b-83e9-54b1-9114-9945ac274319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270193} {:answer/id #uuid "993c66db-4485-5a71-b899-3bd5a69c5cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270194} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1f3ec-4258-589b-82d9-11e84123fd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270195} {:answer/id #uuid "9d22fe07-a608-5ece-ab09-4690c51631d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0fce35b-dea5-5fa0-915b-14d76c22527c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270192} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b47dac2-6abb-5629-98e5-0416c1e1241f" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c976869-dd96-5219-84a7-962a7edc295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115511" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463b6d4c-409f-55ba-b1f6-69f6d9773939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 − \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270417} {:answer/id #uuid "d9182720-44d8-5d56-bdf5-2439aa2c0463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos x − 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270418} {:answer/id #uuid "11cd5682-9da4-5615-b3a5-54f04a88f23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270419} {:answer/id #uuid "afa22122-5058-57c5-9441-e138d829ef82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270420} {:answer/id #uuid "c03774a8-b129-5c10-b7e5-c6274b9465cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x − 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa22122-5058-57c5-9441-e138d829ef82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270420} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f2c4aa7-a316-5bc3-b0f0-430c759b4ac7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a918518-91f1-50bb-bef6-5c2c5dd04e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119865" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c55b1c-2629-5f05-89b5-45e391df353d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)+2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270457} {:answer/id #uuid "62e37908-4e24-5ea2-aba3-d760e0d9d0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270458} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2ae01f-f370-503d-9b9e-b324625c9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270459} {:answer/id #uuid "0b11ab8e-a4d3-5fbd-9394-f2ea808f43bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)+1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270460} {:answer/id #uuid "49deb945-0e38-5092-8e3f-ed774a8bcc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)-2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e37908-4e24-5ea2-aba3-d760e0d9d0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270458} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e4ea2f-f53a-5af1-9229-d78a463f9c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b65dbd4c-66de-5fa9-bf68-86d78bdb1d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-120300" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-11).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-11)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe335eb-dc4a-5ea0-83cf-ad929e19b908" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection and Shifting of a Sine or Cosine Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea6a1c0a-b897-5392-8b63-6dcef02fcfb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270782} {:answer/id #uuid "35dbc459-f8e6-5c06-9333-af1766e7c252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270783} {:answer/id #uuid "bc160c85-41a4-53ff-a6b2-6903f45b3946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270784} {:answer/id #uuid "bc10aae8-da97-5c62-a84b-319ab90d723f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270785} {:answer/id #uuid "6007d829-05c8-5678-a6c6-41861cc0c9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc160c85-41a4-53ff-a6b2-6903f45b3946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270784} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e558b6a-76b4-5c0f-9a7e-65f7da395130" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af276f5b-4246-5be5-bc99-ae92802b70cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-12669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4618627-82f2-504b-ae29-ec11136eae20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270937} {:answer/id #uuid "7e469656-bd75-5579-910a-b16978b23215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270938} {:answer/id #uuid "320cd49a-eb63-5e3d-aff0-d07fd19f7577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270939} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff7a7ff-0478-5b8d-a50e-2cef499bdc25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270940} {:answer/id #uuid "33503006-56a2-51d8-83c8-db4406f65503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320cd49a-eb63-5e3d-aff0-d07fd19f7577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0ad887-88be-52fa-8b2d-8779ddd115b5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4ce9fc-ec03-5271-bd17-eb5db2756338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.663" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271602} {:answer/id #uuid "16badea8-1bce-5a97-87ad-61316b81618a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271603} {:answer/id #uuid "fd97beee-79bc-53a8-ae9e-5e4e1f184e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.613" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271604} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa61d5d-c99b-5393-9e1f-c3a7e0f5c9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271605} {:answer/id #uuid "03dcf9bb-0fdc-562e-a51e-3e04be664a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa61d5d-c99b-5393-9e1f-c3a7e0f5c9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7178a590-d365-59b6-abf3-46595991afe4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ba40cf-c229-5faa-87a1-5d86079dac01" :question/math-academy-id "q-26387" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{9},$ what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${9}^{2y+1}={5}^{-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef898c1e-d9fd-5fc5-a3b2-eec2abe8e15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272664} {:answer/id #uuid "747f9201-514b-5369-b32b-70d05b3dbe38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272665} {:answer/id #uuid "94c0dee0-cade-5849-8e5a-19c0769bab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272666} {:answer/id #uuid "669f7bc5-f47d-5b7a-b784-944f3727762a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272667} {:answer/id #uuid "66f976c1-142e-5ac1-8488-8cd193d35356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c0dee0-cade-5849-8e5a-19c0769bab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272666} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2bdce8-f6d8-51cd-b5d9-d083888cb53c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} {:answer/id #uuid "ac445ed8-952c-5de1-a6c0-5dc70b0c8824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273408} {:answer/id #uuid "0804e507-30be-5121-94cc-9076f9654b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273409} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaa79a7-0886-5ca5-aa46-640b99b4fc38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "200b4d45-b33d-5820-8836-b437c8a6186d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80de19d-031f-542d-9602-c6d7f9e5629d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe0d77e-52ec-55af-86ee-7865dc316245" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Monomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae96aae-1f10-5ac7-9d29-105c275549e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273571} {:answer/id #uuid "d8073d17-352d-5ea3-bcfa-4f1cb00b9517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273572} {:answer/id #uuid "d632c816-a222-5e8e-b9ba-45e2d2ef2cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273573} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed832fe-8708-575c-a106-069f39aca48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273574} {:answer/id #uuid "1a59a83f-214f-5cad-98db-156efd569fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae96aae-1f10-5ac7-9d29-105c275549e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273571} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c3b091d-1720-5e63-b555-a040fcf0b69c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb1d235-c690-5225-8779-e89c17dee989" :question/math-academy-id "q-76311" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $6w,$ $3{w}^{3},$ and $4{w}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three monomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable parts. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,12,18,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,6,9,12,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,8,12,16,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $w,\\,{w}^{3},$ and ${w}^{2},$ we raise $w$ to the largest power that appears in all the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,{w}^{3},{w}^{2})={w}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6w,3{w}^{3},4{w}^{2})=12\\cdot {w}^{3}=12{w}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f783fe8-8f52-5f93-adef-bf4bd030fea0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Vertical Hyperbola in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fe253a0-ed5d-5555-b285-435327a0f166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273676} {:answer/id #uuid "33fb4628-7add-51c2-934b-71d0cf67b7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273677} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3d7ac6-be3e-5b00-8eea-08be847d1662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273678} {:answer/id #uuid "99679d3b-293c-5c7c-bae4-e47e93374f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273679} {:answer/id #uuid "ca192385-d364-5616-b55d-a35d7e2f24fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33fb4628-7add-51c2-934b-71d0cf67b7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273677} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d9fa9ac-68d1-54f9-892f-67d0b3e6e2ed" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa7228ce-645a-52a8-9a40-e535c79572db" :question/math-academy-id "q-78257" :question/problem "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8a39239-61e0-54d7-9d05-0da895761028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+3,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273736} {:answer/id #uuid "32768fed-e28a-50d5-b9c8-81276fca1f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273737} {:answer/id #uuid "6db5bc71-5084-5b70-8071-509fe66c2611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-1,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273738} {:answer/id #uuid "21dce0ec-c390-5f52-baf7-ed7d3a648b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273739} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb0e3f4-4801-57b8-a07c-77a7f32648a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32768fed-e28a-50d5-b9c8-81276fca1f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273737} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac838a6-f659-5dae-ad35-b0552d1a7f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28eb4849-33b4-5a68-b600-726a71471105" :question/math-academy-id "q-79870" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}(-3x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(8) & ={log}_{4}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(5) & ={log}_{4}(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$"}]}
